<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), or People's Liberation Front, is a Marxist party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> founded in 1965 by Rohana Wijeweera. It led armed uprisings in 1971 and 1987–1989, both crushed by the state with heavy loss of life. Later entering parliamentary politics, it formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-power">National People's Power</a> alliance, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anura-kumara-dissanayake">Anura Kumara Dissanayake</a> won the presidency in 2024.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janatha-vimukthi-peramuna.jpg" alt="Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PLATEL · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janatha_Vimukthi_Peramuna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded to lead a communist revolution at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> already had four other leftist parties, among them the Lanka Sama Samaja Party and the Communist Party of Sri Lanka. Rohana Wijeweera's father, a Communist Party activist, had been paralysed after an assault by UNP members during an election campaign. The party then arranged a scholarship for the son at the Patrice Lumumba Friendship University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where he read Marx, Engels and Lenin; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a> he joined the pro-Chinese Ceylon Communist Party (Maoist).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In March 1971 an accidental explosion led police to fifty-eight bombs in a hut at Nelundeniya, and Wijeweera was arrested. The other leaders began the uprising at 11:00 p.m. on 5 April. The young and poorly armed insurgents held parts of the southern and central provinces, but within two weeks the government had regained almost all of them, and the army and police were widely seen to have used excessive force. Wijeweera received a twenty-year sentence.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-r-jayewardene">J. R. Jayewardene</a> released him in 1977, and in the 1982 presidential election he won 4.16% of the vote. After the Indo-Sri Lanka Accord the party rose again in 1987, enforcing general strikes by violence and killing probably thousands of people. Government forces captured and killed Wijeweera and his deputy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> in November 1989, amid credible accusations of brutality and extrajudicial methods; official and unofficial reports put the death toll of the insurgency above 60,000.</p><p>Rebuilt under Somawansa Amarasinghe, the party joined the United People's Freedom Alliance government in 2004 and later left it. From 2019 it contested elections within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-power">National People's Power</a> coalition, which won 159 seats in the parliamentary elections of 2024.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party's first ideology drew on Maoist stress on the rural peasantry, Guevarist views of armed insurrection and Trotskyist criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>. In 1983 it adopted Jathika Chintanaya, a national ideology that moved it towards Sinhalese Buddhist nationalism and opposition to Tamil demands. Amarasinghe later abandoned that line for a pragmatic socialism while the party remained officially Marxist-Leninist. In the early 1970s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> gave it training, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> expelled 18 North Koreans and cut diplomatic ties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A youth insurrection by the <b>JVP</b> was suppressed in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li><li><blockquote>Indian peacekeepers intervened from 1987 to 1990, and a second <b>JVP</b> insurrection was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></li></ul></section>
